WEBVTT

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.202
Picture a big bowl of pasta.

00:00:02.862 --> 00:00:04.784
Within an hour, glucose from that meal is

00:00:04.944 --> 00:00:06.165
pouring into your blood.

00:00:06.946 --> 00:00:08.708
Then you sleep for eight hours or more

00:00:08.768 --> 00:00:11.370
without eating, and your brain keeps using glucose

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:11.910
all night.

00:00:12.731 --> 00:00:14.973
Yet a blood test at breakfast would usually

00:00:14.993 --> 00:00:16.755
show glucose back in its normal range.

00:00:17.735 --> 00:00:20.097
So what pulls glucose down after the meal,

00:00:20.598 --> 00:00:22.059
and what pushes it back up while you

00:00:22.079 --> 00:00:22.299
sleep?

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:26.521
Back to the pasta.

00:00:26.541 --> 00:00:30.143
As glucose rises, beta cells release more insulin,

00:00:30.543 --> 00:00:32.044
and it travels in the blood to cells

00:00:32.324 --> 00:00:33.025
all over the body.

00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:36.207
In muscle and fat cells, insulin binds to

00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:37.668
receptors on the cell surface.

00:00:38.328 --> 00:00:42.130
That signal moves extra glucose transporters, proteins that

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:45.112
let glucose through, from inside the cell into

00:00:45.152 --> 00:00:45.733
its membrane.

00:00:46.273 --> 00:00:49.055
More doors open, so glucose moves in faster.

00:00:49.935 --> 00:00:53.257
In the liver, insulin switches on glycogenesis, which

00:00:53.297 --> 00:00:54.558
means building glycogen:

00:00:55.198 --> 00:00:57.460
glucose is joined into chains and stored.

00:01:00.002 --> 00:01:01.031
Two exam questions.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:02.002
First.

00:01:02.787 --> 00:01:04.551
A student eats breakfast at seven,

00:01:05.093 --> 00:01:07.619
skips lunch, and runs a cross-country race at

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:08.763
two in the afternoon.

00:01:09.406 --> 00:01:10.991
Their blood glucose begins to fall.

00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:14.675
Construct a negative feedback loop that returns blood

00:01:14.695 --> 00:01:17.057
glucose to normal, and explain the role of

00:01:17.097 --> 00:01:17.418
the liver.

00:01:18.241 --> 00:01:19.253
Pause here to answer.
